[{"_1":2},"llms/mdx",{"_3":4},"data","# hawkBit mode (/docs/pr-preview/pr-56/platform/updates/hawkbit-mode)\n\n\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  In `hawkbit` mode, Lamassu Updates serves the same `/updates/v1` API on top of an [Eclipse hawkBit](https://www.eclipse.org/hawkbit/) server instead of its own Postgres, WFX and SymKMS-based backend. The service becomes an **adapter**: hawkBit owns packs, modules, rollouts and jobs and is read live on every request, and devices talk to hawkBit directly.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Use it when hawkBit is already your fleet's update backend, or to compare the two backends. To choose between them, see the [overview](/docs/platform/updates/overview).\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```mermaid\n  flowchart LR\n    operator[\"Dashboard / API clients\"] --> adapter[\"Lamassu Updates<br/>(hawkbit mode)\"]\n    adapter -->|Management API| hawkbit[\"Eclipse hawkBit\"]\n    adapter --> pg[(\"Postgres<br/>updates\")]\n    adapter -.->|DMS API, KMS, SymKMS| lamassu[\"Lamassu IoT\"]\n    device[\"Device (SWUpdate)\"] -->|DDI poll| hawkbit\n    device -->|artifact URL| adapter\n    adapter -.->|302 signed URL| device\n    device -->|signed GET| s3[(\"S3 store\")]\n    hawkbit -->|stores artifacts| s3\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Prerequisites [#prerequisites]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  * A reachable hawkBit server (1.x) and its Management API credentials.\n  * Postgres for this service. See [What lives where](#what-lives-where).\n  * Devices that poll hawkBit through SWUpdate's `suricatta` module. With `dms_endpoint` set, the service creates the hawkBit targets of a group's devices and tags them itself (see [Devices and targets](#devices-and-targets)). Without it, you register and tag them in hawkBit.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Enable it [#enable-it]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```yaml\n  mode: hawkbit\n  hawkbit:\n    base_url: \"http://hawkbit:8080\"      # hawkBit's Management API\n    username: \"admin\"\n    password: \"<hawkbit admin password>\"\n    tenant: \"\"                           # DDI tenant path segment; defaults to DEFAULT\n  storage:                               # required: Postgres of this service\n    provider: postgres\n    hostname: postgres\n    port: 5432\n    username: postgres\n    password: \"<password>\"\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  `mode` is `native` when unset, so existing configurations are unaffected. Startup fails if `hawkbit.base_url` or `storage.hostname` is empty. An unreachable hawkBit does not stop startup: it shows up as errors on requests.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  To build deliverables in this mode, add the pieces you need on top. All are read once at start-up:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  | You want                               | Set                                                                                                           |\n  | -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n  | `swu-build` modules (assembled `.swu`) | `swugenerator_path`                                                                                           |\n  | Signing with the Lamassu KMS           | also `sign_script_path` and `kms_endpoint` (raw modules need only `kms_endpoint`)                             |\n  | Shared-key encryption                  | `symkms_endpoint`, plus the `symkms` block or `sym_keys_dir` so the process registers the `/symkms/v1` routes |\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  See the [configuration reference](/docs/platform/updates/reference/configuration) and [Signing and encryption](/docs/platform/updates/signing-and-encryption). Without these keys, `GET /updates/v1/capabilities` reports `artifact_encryption: false` and the dashboard hides the encryption selector.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  <Callout type=\"info\" title=\"Which port is the adapter on?\">\n    The API listens on `app.server.port`, and clients choose it. The dashboard reads its API URL from `LAMASSU_UPDATES_API`, so point that at the instance that runs the mode you want. If you run a native and a `hawkbit` instance side by side, give them different ports, and set each instance's `symkms_endpoint` to **its own** port: it points at the same process.\n  </Callout>\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Run it locally [#run-it-locally]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  The repository's `docker-compose.yml` starts a hawkBit (host port `8090`, backed by the `hawkbit` Postgres database) and the Postgres both services use:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```bash\n  # docker-compose.yml needs this network to exist (skip if you already created it).\n  docker network create ota-net\n\n  docker compose up -d postgres hawkbit\n\n  # Wait until hawkBit is ready. The first boot runs its migrations and takes about 30 seconds.\n  until curl -sf -u \"admin:${HAWKBIT_ADMIN_PASSWORD:-admin}\" 'http://localhost:8090/rest/v1/targets?limit=1' >/dev/null; do sleep 2; done\n\n  OTA_CONFIG_FILE=./config-hawkbit.yml go run ./cmd/monolithic\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  `config-hawkbit.yml` contains values for one developer machine: absolute paths for `swugenerator_path`, `sign_script_path` and the SymKMS directories, and a LAN address for `kms_endpoint`. Edit them first, and point them at paths the service user can create. The file has no `dms_endpoint`: add the URL of your Lamassu Device Manager, or the service has no device groups to launch to.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Check the backend:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```bash\n  curl -s localhost:10090/updates/v1/capabilities\n  # {\"backend\":\"hawkbit\",\"supported\":{...},\"unsupported\":[...]}\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Create and build a pack [#create-and-build-a-pack]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  A hawkBit distribution set can only link **built** software modules, so the order differs from native mode. The flow is catalog-first:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  <Steps>\n    <Step>\n      Create the module in the catalog [#create-the-module-in-the-catalog]\n\n      `POST /updates/v1/softwaremodules` with its `type`, `name`, `version` and `delivery_intent`.\n    </Step>\n\n    <Step>\n      Upload its files [#upload-its-files]\n\n      `POST /updates/v1/softwaremodules/by-id/:moduleID/artifacts`. This route streams the file to hawkBit.\n    </Step>\n\n    <Step>\n      Make it built [#make-it-built]\n\n      How depends on the delivery intent (next table).\n    </Step>\n\n    <Step>\n      Create the pack from the built modules [#create-the-pack-from-the-built-modules]\n\n      `POST /updates/v1/groups/:groupID/distribution-sets` with `modules: [{\"source_module_id\": \"<id>\"}]`. The pack is created already built.\n    </Step>\n  </Steps>\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  | `delivery_intent` | How the module becomes built                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      | Joins packs with             |\n  | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------- |\n  | `swu-build`       | Upload its sw-description with `POST /softwaremodules/by-id/:moduleID/descriptor`, then `POST /softwaremodules/by-id/:moduleID/build`. Needs `swugenerator_path`: without it the build answers `501`. Signing and encryption fields go in the build body.                                         | `swu` (default) or `non-swu` |\n  | `swu-prebuilt`    | Upload the finished `.swu`: uploading it builds the module. Calling build answers `400`.                                                                                                                                                                                                          | `swu`                        |\n  | `raw`             | `POST .../build` encrypts (shared key) and/or signs the files; with neither, the files ship unchanged and the service logs a warning. Several files are archived into `<name>.tar.gz`. An encrypted deliverable gets the `.enc` suffix; a signature adds `<file>.sha256` and `<file>.sha256.sig`. | `non-swu`                    |\n  | `undecided`       | Cannot be built or linked. Choose an intent first with `PUT /softwaremodules/by-id/:moduleID`.                                                                                                                                                                                                    | none                         |\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  A build **replaces** the module's input files with the deliverable, so a built module cannot be built again: release a new version of the module.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Operations that make no sense for a built, frozen pack answer as follows:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  | Request                                                                                            | Answer                                                                                          |\n  | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n  | `POST .../distribution-sets/:name/swu` and `.../package`                                           | `501`: build the modules in the catalog instead.                                                |\n  | `.../swu/upload` and `.../package/upload`                                                          | `409`: a hawkBit pack is always already built.                                                  |\n  | Pack-scoped artifact link, sw-description upload, module descriptor, module build, artifact delete | `400`: a linked module is frozen. Unlink it, change and build it in the catalog, link it again. |\n  | Creating a module inside a pack                                                                    | Not possible. Create it in the catalog.                                                         |\n  | Changing the composition of a pack already assigned to devices                                     | `400`: create a new version.                                                                    |\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  See the [publish and roll out guide](/docs/platform/updates/guides/publish-and-roll-out) for the full procedure.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  How it maps [#how-it-maps]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  | Lamassu Updates     | hawkBit                                                      |\n  | ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n  | Update pack version | Distribution set. Every version is its own distribution set. |\n  | Software module     | Software module                                              |\n  | Module artifact     | Software module artifact                                     |\n  | Device              | Target. The controller ID is the device ID.                  |\n  | Device group        | Target tag `group:<id>`                                      |\n  | Launch campaign     | Rollout (static)                                             |\n  | Per-device job      | Rollout action                                               |\n  | Scheduled launch    | The rollout's start time (hawkBit's scheduler starts it)     |\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  What lives where [#what-lives-where]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  hawkBit stores packs, rollouts and jobs. The service still needs Postgres (the `updates` database) for what hawkBit has no equivalent of: staged artifact uploads and aliases, sw-descriptions, signed set manifests, cancelled rollouts and declared latest-version pins. Keeping them in Postgres, not in memory, is what makes running several replicas safe.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  * **sw-descriptions** live in Postgres, not in hawkBit metadata, because hawkBit caps a metadata value at 4000 characters. Descriptors written there by older versions are still read.\n  * **Rows are scoped to a hawkBit instance.** hawkBit ids repeat across servers and after its database is wiped, so the service tags the hawkBit it talks to with a marker (a distribution-set tag named `lamassu:instance`) and ignores rows that belong to another instance. Do not delete that tag.\n  * **Staged uploads are heavy.** `POST /updates/v1/artifacts` (the global artifact upload) reads the whole file into memory and keeps it in Postgres for 30 minutes unless a pack links it, and hawkBit mode refuses to link files to packs. For large files, always upload through the catalog route of the previous section, which streams to hawkBit.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Devices and targets [#devices-and-targets]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  * Devices poll hawkBit, not this service. A pack launched against a hawkBit with no registered targets is never downloaded by anything.\n  * With `dms_endpoint` set, every read of a group's devices creates the missing targets (controller ID = device ID) and tags them `group:<id>`. A device that cannot be reconciled is excluded from that read and logged. Without `dms_endpoint`, groups are the existing `group:<id>` tags.\n  * The first launch with a `test_device_id` (canary) fails with `test device X cannot act as this group's canary` until the group's devices exist in hawkBit. Run a dry run first (`POST .../launch?dry_run=true`), which registers them.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Device authentication in production [#device-authentication-in-production]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  hawkBit decides how devices authenticate, not this service. hawkBit 1.1.0 has no anonymous mode: a deployment must enable at least one of the target security token, the gateway security token, or the reverse-proxy header authenticator (`X-Controller-Id` plus `X-Authority`). Pick one and enable it as a hawkBit tenant setting; an installation where all of them are off answers `401` to every device. Recommended for production:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  * Terminate device mTLS at a gateway, have it set the header authenticator's headers from the device certificate, and let hawkBit trust those headers only from the gateway's addresses (hawkBit's trusted-proxy setting). Anything that can reach hawkBit directly could otherwise impersonate any device.\n  * Or enable target security tokens (`authentication.targettoken.enabled`) and configure each device with its own token; SWUpdate sends it as `Authorization: TargetToken <token>`.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Never expose the Management API to devices. Use a strong password for the Management API user (`HAWKBIT_SECURITY_USER_ADMIN_PASSWORD` in the image) and serve it over HTTPS. If the gateway publishes the DDI under a path prefix, set `hawkbit.ddi_url` to the public DDI URL so the artifact links devices receive are reachable.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Artifact storage and downloads [#artifact-storage-and-downloads]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  By default hawkBit stores artifact bytes on its own filesystem. The `docker/hawkbit` directory of the repository builds a hawkBit image with an **S3 artifact storage plugin**, selected with `HAWKBIT_ARTIFACT_STORAGE=s3` (`fs` is the default) and the `HAWKBIT_ARTIFACT_S3_*` variables (bucket, endpoint, region, path-style access, key prefix; credentials come from the AWS default chain). hawkBit refuses to start if S3 is selected but the bucket is unreachable: it never falls back to local disk silently. Metadata stays in Postgres either way. The plugin and its build and test instructions are in [`docker/hawkbit`](https://github.com/lamassuiot/ota/tree/master/docker/hawkbit); the image is built locally and is not published to a registry.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  With the plugin and `hawkbit.artifact_store.presign` configured, downloads of module artifacts are answered `302` to a short-lived signed URL of the bucket instead of streaming through hawkBit or this service. Devices keep the artifact link hawkBit gave them: the adapter serves that one DDI route (`<ddi path>/:tenant/controller/v1/:controllerId/softwaremodules/:id/artifacts/:filename`), checks that the module is assigned to or installed on that device (otherwise `404`) and redirects. For this to work, the gateway must route that path to Lamassu Updates and the rest of the DDI to hawkBit. See [Configuration](/docs/platform/updates/reference/configuration#presigned-downloads-presign) and [Security](/docs/platform/updates/security#presigned-download-urls).\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  hawkBit has its own artifact limits. The image of this repository raises them to 11 GiB per file and 1 TiB in total, to match the default `max_artifact_size_mb` of 10 GiB (`SPRING_SERVLET_MULTIPART_MAXFILESIZE`, `SPRING_SERVLET_MULTIPART_MAXREQUESTSIZE`, `HAWKBIT_SERVER_SECURITY_DOS_MAXARTIFACTSIZE` and `HAWKBIT_SERVER_SECURITY_DOS_MAXARTIFACTSTORAGE`). With stock hawkBit the limit is 1 GiB per file and 20 GiB in total. hawkBit writes an upload to a temporary file first, so give it that much free disk.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  The hawkBit image sizes its JVM with the environment variables `X_MS` and `X_MX` (default `768m` each), plus `XX_MAX_METASPACE_SIZE`, `XSS` and `GC`. `JAVA_OPTS` does not set the heap. Lowering `X_MX` lowers the ceiling, not the steady memory use.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  What differs from native mode [#what-differs-from-native-mode]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  | Area               | `hawkbit` mode                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n  | ------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n  | Who devices poll   | hawkBit (DDI), not this service.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n  | Capabilities       | Not every native feature exists. `GET /updates/v1/capabilities` lists what the active backend supports: in `hawkbit` mode there are no custom workflows, no strategy editing after creation, no early completion of a campaign, no adding devices to a running campaign, no push events and no per-device encryption. See [Capabilities](/docs/platform/updates/reference/capabilities). |\n  | Encryption         | **Shared key only.** A distribution set serves identical artifacts to every target, so there is nowhere to put a different payload per device. Use `native` mode for per-device encryption.                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n  | Multi-module packs | Each module has its own deliverable, so a pack with several modules installs as **several independent SWUpdate transactions**. SWUpdate cannot roll back across several `.swu` files. See [Concepts](/docs/platform/updates/concepts).                                                                                                                                                   |\n  | Names              | hawkBit soft-deletes software modules, so the name and version of a module that was ever assigned to a device are taken forever. Bump the version.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n  | Launch strategy    | Read back from the rollout's groups, so a fixed batch can read back as a percentage. It cannot be edited after creation.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n  | Concurrent changes | Building or changing the same module from two requests answers `409` (`software module N is being built or changed by another request`).                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n  | Scaling            | hawkBit is its own service with its own database. The Helm chart of this repository does not deploy or manage it.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Scaling [#scaling]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  In `hawkbit` mode nothing is elected and there are no local files to share, so any number of replicas can run: state is in Postgres and hawkBit, and module and launch operations take Postgres locks. If you use shared-key encryption, put SymKMS key storage (`symkms.storage`) on shared storage or S3. See [Operations](/docs/platform/updates/operations#scale-out).\n</div>\n"]
